{# One resolution per render, shared by the <style> block below and the
   brand bar in <body> — asking twice would read the same row twice. #}
{% set brand = branding() %}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{ app.request.locale|default('en') }}">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
        <title>{% block title %}PracticePerfect{% endblock %}</title>
        <link rel="icon" href="{{ asset('images/favicon.svg') }}" type="image/svg+xml">

        {% block stylesheets %}{% endblock %}

        {% block javascripts %}
            {% block importmap %}{{ importmap('app') }}{% endblock %}
        {% endblock %}

        {#
            Runtime branding layer. These five properties are the ONLY
            runtime-overridable custom properties in the system: a trainer's
            brand ships without a deploy because it is a value read at request
            time, never a build input.

            See specs/architect-architecture.md § "White-label branding as a
            runtime value" and specs/frontend-design-spec.md § "Design tokens".

            BrandingProvider resolves the active tenant's colour — including
            on public landing pages, where TenantFromPublicCode has already
            resolved the trainer from the code in the URL — and derives the
            ramp server-side. With no tenant it returns the platform default,
            so this block is unconditionally present and app.css's own
            var(--brand-primary-rgb) references never resolve to nothing.

            Every value interpolated here is machine-derived from a hex the
            entity validated as #RRGGBB; no free text reaches the stylesheet.
            The CSP nonce attaches here once the security headers land.

            Nothing else may be emitted inline, and nothing in app.css may
            reference a trainer's raw hex except through these five names.
        #}
        {% block branding %}
        <style>
            :root {
                --brand-primary: {{ brand.primaryHex }};
                --brand-primary-soft: {{ brand.primarySoftHex }};
                --brand-primary-deep: {{ brand.primaryDeepHex }};
                --brand-primary-rgb: {{ brand.primaryRgb }};
                --brand-on-primary: {{ brand.onPrimaryHex }};
            }
        </style>
        {% endblock %}
    </head>
    <body>
        <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to main content</a>

        {#
            AC-01-34: a sticky, color-coded banner on every page for the
            duration of an impersonation session. Driven entirely from
            IS_IMPERSONATOR (Symfony's own switch_user marker role) rather
            than a bespoke flag, so it can never drift out of sync with the
            actual security token — specs/api-designer-spec.md
            "Impersonation — a whole session, one identity swap".
        #}
        {% if is_granted('IS_IMPERSONATOR') %}
            <div class="impersonation-banner" role="alert">
                <span>Viewing as {{ app.user.email }}</span>
                <form method="post" action="{{ path('impersonation_exit') }}">
                    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('impersonation-exit') }}">
                    <button type="submit">Exit Impersonation</button>
                </form>
            </div>
        {% endif %}

        {#
            AC-01-60: "Logo visible to: Trainer's players, coaches, parents"
            — so it belongs on every page they can reach, not only on the
            trainer's own settings screen where it was previously the single
            place a logo appeared at all.

            Rendered through <img>, never inlined, so an uploaded SVG cannot
            execute in this origin (architect-architecture.md). The platform's
            own logo stands in until a trainer uploads theirs, which is why
            BrandingProvider always resolves something.

            The business name lives in the alt attribute rather than as page
            copy: this bar is on every page, and a trainer's name repeated as
            visible text on all of them would be noise — and would quietly
            satisfy any test that only asks whether the name appears
            somewhere on a page.
        #}
        {#
            The navigation lives here, beside the logo, because it has to be
            on every page rather than on the one screen that happened to
            build it. Its own partial carries the reasoning.

            The logo doubles as the way home once there is a home to go to;
            an anonymous visitor on a landing page gets the mark without a
            link. The alt text stays the plain business name — that is the
            link's accessible name, and "logo, link" reading as "home" is the
            convention. The literal way home is the nav's own Dashboard link,
            which is what makes this an affordance rather than the only route.
        #}
        {% block header %}
            <header class="brand-bar">
                {% if app.user %}
                    <a class="brand-bar__home" href="{{ path('app_dashboard') }}">
                        <img class="brand-bar__logo"
                             src="{{ brand.logoPath ?? asset('images/default_logo.svg') }}"
                             alt="{{ brand.businessName }}"
                             height="32">
                    </a>
                {% else %}
                    <img class="brand-bar__logo"
                         src="{{ brand.logoPath ?? asset('images/default_logo.svg') }}"
                         alt="{{ brand.businessName }}"
                         height="32">
                {% endif %}
            </header>

            {{ include('platform/_navigation.html.twig') }}
        {% endblock %}

        <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
            {# AC-01-49 and every other "...with a confirmation message"
               criterion: flash messages set via addFlash() render here, once,
               for every page in the application. #}
            {% for type, messages in app.flashes %}
                {% for message in messages %}
                    <p role="status" class="flash flash--{{ type }}">{{ message }}</p>
                {% endfor %}
            {% endfor %}

            {% block body %}{% endblock %}
        </main>

        {% block footer %}{% endblock %}
    </body>
</html>
